{
  "dom_id": "step-q313343",
  "name": "Question 17",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/assets/204afb23fc438b047c72d6233b59b70c4741ee16a30a57c084682a3d14fd2188.png)\n\nA thin soap film is stretching over time. The graph of its surface area $A(t)$, in square centimeters, after $t$ minutes is shown above. Approximate the rate of change of the surface area at $t=2$ using a forward difference with step size $h=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nAfter $2$ minutes, the surface area increases at a rate of {{field-1}} ${\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$",
  "worked_solution": "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{A}^{\u2032}(t)\u2248\\frac{A(t+h)-A(t)}{h},\n$$\n\nat the time $t=2$ with step size $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{\u2032}(2) & \u2248\\frac{A(2+2)-A(2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{A(4)-A(2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{40-10}{2} \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after $2$ minutes, the surface area changes at a rate of approximately $15\\text{\u00a0}{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$",
